## C# / .NET Rules

### C# Style
- Use modern collection expressions `[]` instead of `new List<T> {}` or `new T[] {}`
- Naming: `PascalCase` for classes/methods/properties; `camelCase` for locals/parameters

### FastEndpoints Pattern
- Endpoints inherit `Ep.Req<TRequest>.Res<TResponse>` (or `Ep.NoReq.Res<T>`)
- Always set `.WithName("camelCase")` — drives the generated API client operation name
- Place endpoints in `[Context].Application/Endpoints/`

### Response Envelope Pattern
All responses use an envelope — never return raw models:
- Single: `GetItemResponse { Item: ItemResponseModel }`
- List: `GetItemsResponse { Items: List<ItemResponseModel> }`
- DTO naming: `___Request`/`___Response` for I/O, `___ResponseModel` for GET data
- Shared cross-module models go in `[Module].Public/EndpointContracts/`

### Bounded Context Structure
```
[Context].Domain/          — entities, value objects
[Context].Application/     — FastEndpoints, services, mapping
[Context].Infrastructure/  — EF DbContext, migrations
[Context].Public/          — DTOs for cross-module use only
```
Modules never reference each other directly — only via `.Public`. APIs output camelCase JSON.

### EF Core Migrations
Never create migration files manually. Always use:
```powershell
dotnet ef migrations add MigrationName `
  --project [Module].Infrastructure `
  --startup-project [Project].MigrationService `
  --context [Module]DbContext
```
If it fails: check DbContext constructor, `ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly`, entity configs, EF packages, MigrationService references, and compilation errors.

### Query Performance
Avoid N+1 queries — use `.Include()` or `.Select()` projections.
